  "outcome": "resolved",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, I checked your laptop (BL-LT-0106) and found the likely reason it's slow: the drive is almost full. Only about 9 GB of 256 GB is free (3.5%), and Windows starts slowing down once free space drops below about 10%. Here's how to free up space:\n1. Empty your Recycle Bin and clear out your Downloads folder.\n2. Go to Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files. Remove the temporary files and any old Windows Update files.\n3. Turn on OneDrive Files On-Demand. Right-click the OneDrive cloud icon, then go to Settings > Sync and back up > Advanced settings. This keeps synced files in the cloud until you open them.\n4. Uninstall any apps you no longer use.\n5. Do a full restart: Start > Power > Restart. Just closing the lid doesn't count. After that, install any pending Windows updates and restart again.\nIf it's still slow after all this, or you still have less than about 10% free space, reply to this ticket and we'll look into it further. Please also tell us if you get any blue screens or crashes.",
  "internal_note": "Checked the user's account and BL-LT-0106. The device is assigned to leo.martins, is online, and runs Windows 11 Pro. Disk is 256 GB total with 9 GB free (3.5%), and the device has an active low-disk-space warning. That is well under the 10% threshold in KB-017 and is the most likely cause of the slowness. Gave the user the KB-017 cleanup steps and the KB-019 restart and update steps. Did not ask about last restart time or recent installs because the disk cause is clear. If free space is still under 10% after cleanup, or the laptop is still slow after restart and updates, escalate to Tier 2 per KB-017/KB-019 (may need a bigger drive, hardware check, or replacement). No technician action needed at this point.",
